    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d484">

                <pb facs="0891" n="859" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v06/pg859"/>

                <head>484. Memorandum of a Conference With the President, White House, Washington,
                    March 17, 1960, 11 a.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d484-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1960-03-17T11:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 17, 1960,
                                11 a.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>OTHERS PRESENT</label>
                    <item>Secretary Herter</item>
                    <item>General Goodpaster</item>
                </list>
                <p>Mr. Herter said it was the view of the State Department that Ambassador Bonsal
                    should be sent back to Cuba. He believes that this is the last time Bonsal
                    should return if the Cubans involve him in their charges and plots in the
                    future. He showed the President a proposed message to our chargé in Cuba,
                    including the text of a proposed press release.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d484-f2"/>
                    The President said he agreed that we might as well get Bonsal back in Havana, to
                    do whatever we can with a very unsatisfactory situation. Mr. Herter said that
                    the South Americans are reacting very favorably to the restrained and correct
                    actions being taken by the United States in this whole matter, and he thought
                    Bonsal’s return would help this.</p>
                <p>[Here follows discussion of matters unrelated to Cuba.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
